WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
It's fast skating, hip checking, high scoring action.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.520
Lead your team into center ice over the opponent's blue line.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.400
Pass over to the point and set up for the tip in.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.560
Score ice hockey.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:33.240
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:34.840
I'm Mike. I'm Joe.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.280
I'm Sean. And I'm Sam.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.000
Guys, how we doing? Excellent.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.700
Yeah, and yeah.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.180
Collectively, good.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:42.640
Okay, good. Now that I heard everyone else talking.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:43.460
We voted before.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:44.520
Yeah, I wasn't sure, but now I know.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.540
We're good. Feeling good is in the essential games list.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:47.860
Okay, cool. Great.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.240
Sean, specifically, how are you feeling?

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.660
Because we're doing an ice hockey episode and that's kinda your jam.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.160
Yeah, I mean, it's also kinda your jam, Mike.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.500
Oh, okay. I kinda think of you as the guy who got me more into ice hockey.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.020
Really? Yeah. Oh, that makes me feel so much better.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.900
Fantastic. Well, yeah, no, I mean, I love ice hockey.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.460
I was very intrigued to see how this would be done here.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.920
And, you know, it happens.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.000
Yeah, absolutely. And Sam, you had a funny way to describe ice hockey to me earlier today.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:16.560
Yeah, it's like cold tennis.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.220
Yeah. Wrong.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.920
Yeah. Elaborate.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.460
Cold soccer is better.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.480
Nah. Oh, okay.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.360
Yeah. It's like cold billiards.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.540
Technically, they could play soccer in the cold.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.200
You don't really play tennis when it's cold.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.560
It's like cold croquet.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.380
Right. Sure. If it's cold and there's tennis, then they're just like, they're indoors.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.440
You know? And then it might as well be hockey.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.080
Yeah, but hockey is indoors. It can be outdoors, though.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.700
Joe, you have a brother who plays hockey.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:43.900
I have a brother who plays hockey.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:45.100
Yeah. I mean, played hockey.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.740
He's older than me. It'd be cool if we just leave it in big.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.240
Yeah. He's a hockey coach, I think, right now.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:51.580
Very cool. Yeah. All right.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.780
So, you can see that this episode is gonna be, like, you know, you're dealing with a lot

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.000
of experts. Yeah.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.520
So, we're gonna definitely be the final authority on ice hockey and all future hockey games that

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:06.140
come to Nintendo. But, uh, we should probably talk real fast about development because I managed to just,

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.620
like, scoop in, like, one development fact.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.540
Usually, with these kinds of games, you don't get anything.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:13.120
It's a sports game made by Nintendo.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.260
You know, it just appeared one day in the headquarters.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.120
Yeah. And it's like, oh, we should release this.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.200
You know? Like, there's nothing on baseball.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:22.720
There's nothing on tennis.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:26.040
There's nothing on volleyball. So, I thought it was nice that I could get that.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:33.440
Hideki Kono, who's the designer for ice hockey, later went on to direct other Nintendo titles

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:39.360
like Super Mario Kart, Super Mario World 2 Yoshi's Island, and the Luigi Mansion series.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:40.560
Wow. That is impressive.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:47.060
It makes me wonder if, like, is he a hockey fan that just wanted to do that, or did he

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:49.960
get assigned to make ice hockey?

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.200
Well, it kind of feels like, you know, just to recap the sports games we've gotten from

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.700
Nintendo so far, they haven't done any repeats yet.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:03:01.540
Yeah. So, it kind of feels like, oh, we forgot about that other fourth major sport in North America.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.640
Yeah. So, we should make that one.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:09.140
Some would call it the first minor sport, but that hurts my feelings, so.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:14.120
Yeah. And what other, like, sports are they missing, really, at this point, now that ice hockey's

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.980
been fulfilled? Well, they got football, they got basketball, they got baseball several times, and they've

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:22.860
got hockey. So, I guess they could go to lacrosse.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.040
So, they went soccer, too. And soccer. Yeah.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:25.740
Yep. Lacrosse.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.780
I got a big uptrend of any lacrosse games in 2019.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:33.160
Cricket. Cricket would be good, and that would hit that European audience.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:34.460
Bowling? Yeah.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:35.740
Bowling would be great, too.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.780
I don't even know if we're going to get a bowling game, to be honest.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:44.900
We bowling. I only like bowling games that have that, like, spinny sphere in the arcade machines.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:50.220
Oh, yeah. Yeah, those are fun. Actually, Wii Sports Bowling is the pinnacle of bowling games.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:51.440
Sure, I forgot about that.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:57.820
You know, like, I'm serious. That game is like, everybody bought Wii Sports just because it came with the console, but everybody

00:03:57.820 --> 00:03:58.920
was playing that bowling one.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:03:59.940
Just so we need cheese.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.720
Yeah, it is. Or throw it behind you and scare everyone.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.220
Right, yeah. Just over and over and over again.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.860
Yeah. Anyway, Sean, I'm going to throw this to you.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.720
Can you tell us the objective of ice hockey?

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:20.560
So, in the game and the sport of ice hockey, your objective is to put the rubber puck into

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:25.660
your opponent's net and make sure it does not go into yours via hockey stick.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:27.100
Sounds great. Six, plural.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:29.160
Well, yeah. Multiple sticks.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.500
You failed to mention that part where it goes on ice.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.520
Oh, yeah. It goes on ice.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:37.240
You wear skates. You must skate.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.320
Yeah, you shouldn't wear shoes.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:44.340
That's probably the first tip I would give to any prospective hockey player.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:53.220
But, I mean, you know, they miss, this game does a very basic job of providing the

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:55.040
rules to this sport.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:58.000
It feels like the baseball version that we got.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.540
Kind of. For baseball, where it was like, you know, if you hit the guy, it's like, it just goes

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:02.540
right through. Yeah.

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:07.600
But this one has a major rule change in that there's five players instead of six.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:13.680
True. I think that that was probably for the best, considering the size of the playing surface

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:14.780
in this game.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:18.980
It would have been crowded with all six players per team.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.160
But they also like made it to the blue line doesn't mean anything.

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:29.320
There's a lot of weird like omissions in the rule book in this game.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:31.720
But, you know, it's still, you know, it's still hockey.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:36.840
Yeah. You forgot to mention that after the face off, it's nonstop mayhem on the ice.

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:38.220
A battle to the end.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:39.620
Will you be the champion?

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.760
That is in the object of the game section of the manual.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.900
Oh, well, that is in the NHL rule book.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:48.680
I forgot about that. A battle to the end.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:56.060
Yeah. It's weird because there's like, you know, hockey, I feel like it is the one that there

00:05:56.060 --> 00:06:00.040
are certain rules that if you're not a fan of the sport, you just won't know, like icing

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:01.080
and stuff like that.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:02.880
And they chose to include that.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:03.840
They put icing in.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:08.480
Yeah. It seems like something that isn't necessary at this level, at this primitive level of

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:09.940
ice. I was surprised to see icing.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:11.400
Right. Like let's have that rule in there.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:16.480
I do think that it is probably one of the more useful rules to put in there because,

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:22.680
you know, you could just keep, you could make your opponent infuriated if you kept icing

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:23.860
the puck. Yep.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:31.100
And team wise, you get to choose between the United States, the Soviet Union, Sweden, Poland,

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:33.640
Canada, and Czechoslovakia.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:35.280
Do you feel like they left anyone out?

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:38.460
Mexico. Good call.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:40.300
Joe, did they leave anyone out?

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:41.760
I think. Antarctica.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:43.440
Yeah. Like a penguin team.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:45.300
Oh, that'd be a cool unlockable.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.400
The continent of Antarctica, it's already frozen.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:48.780
They probably ice skate all the time.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:52.180
Where are the ice climbers as like a bonus unlockable characters?

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:53.580
Like, you know, I'm just getting creative.

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:54.840
I don't know what Nintendo's thinking.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:56.220
Why isn't Mario the ref?

00:06:56.440 --> 00:06:57.440
Who did you have in mind?

00:06:57.800 --> 00:06:58.880
Who did I have in mind?

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:02.940
Uh, honestly, like maybe something like, uh, like Germany or something like that.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.040
Eh, Germany doesn't really do well.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:06.380
Yeah. That's true.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:10.700
Well, in the Japanese version, the Famicom version, uh, they, they actually replaced Sweden

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:13.040
with Japan. Which I think makes sense.

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:16.460
Right. I don't think, you know, like you want to have like, I want to play as that team, but

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:19.540
I don't know about you guys, but when I first started the game up, I did choose USA.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:20.740
Yeah, of course. As the team.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:22.320
Because you have USA.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:27.640
Right. I'm not like a blind patriot, but at the same time, like, I feel like I would pick the

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:29.340
USA. I mean, you saw a miracle.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:30.960
You can't not do that.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.320
Yeah. You always hit the big USA versus Soviet Union.

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:35.040
That's, that's what I did for my first match.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:38.440
Absolutely. Uh, so let's just get into the gameplay.

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:41.620
Why not? Uh, there's, there's a couple of different types of players.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:44.280
Sam, would you like to break down for us these different players?

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:51.820
I'd love to. Uh, so you got the skinny ones, which go really fast, but don't shoot very hard and get tackled

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:53.220
very easily. Tackled.

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:54.800
Not checked. Checked.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:55.880
I don't know the terminology.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:57.040
Well, tackle, no man.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:58.240
I agree with that.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:07:59.320
I mean, technically it's a tackle, I guess.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:04.940
Uh, then you got the, the stocky ones, which don't move as quickly, but they have really

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:11.840
good shots and will tackle or check harder and won't fall as easily.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:15.060
And then you just got your average Joes, which are just kind of right in the middle.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.440
I was kind of surprised at the average Joe thing.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:21.920
Like they couldn't really come up with like a triangle system of like three things.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:26.060
Like, like, you know, like this guy's good at the face offs, but he's bad at shooting,

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:28.700
but he's also good at the other thing.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:29.740
And then just swap those.

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:33.480
I didn't really think about that. The average thing was like, I don't know, after playing around with it for a little while,

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:35.740
I figured out it's just like, yeah, just pick the average guys all around.

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:38.540
Cause then you're pretty much just like safe in all areas.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:43.220
Cause I don't know about you guys, but controlling in this game, like who you want to pass the puck

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:44.460
to. It's kind of messed up.

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:47.540
Yeah. It's kind of messed up. I just want to make sure I have the same guy all the time.

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:55.800
Yeah. Um, I think that, um, I actually did like the mix of different kinds of players.

00:08:56.120 --> 00:09:00.920
Um, because you do get to choose the makeup, uh, and like you get to choose the way that

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:01.720
you want to play the game.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:07.420
Um, you, I was considering just picking a bunch of bruisers and just steamrolling everybody.

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:13.320
But I thought that like, no, I like that the skinny guy is, can just be like a face off

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:16.500
dude. And then you just win every face off with that guy.

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:18.600
I don't know. It was also faster too.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:20.220
It's faster. You can set them up.

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:23.060
Um, no, it was cool.

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:29.100
Right. And I think just, just the option to have different types of players available is like, that's

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:32.420
good progress on Nintendo's end to, to keep figuring out like things they should add

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:33.240
to these sports games.

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.180
Cause it's like one thing to be able to choose teams.

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:40.040
Mm hmm. And then it's like, you can actually customize your, your roster is, is something that we

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:41.460
didn't see in their previous sports games.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:45.340
So I thought that was neat. And then it's only like a, a, a hop, skip and a step.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:47.880
And then you've got people arguing about Madden ratings, you know?

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:50.780
So. Right. Yeah. When is too much detail?

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:52.880
When, when you hit that?

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:56.580
I was thinking it's kind of like backyard baseball or backyard hockey, I guess, in this

00:09:56.580 --> 00:10:01.800
case with, you know, very basic stats that some people are better at, at some things

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:02.680
than other people.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:05.000
You know, it seems like it's kind of approaching that direction.

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:09.660
Which again is something I agree with for, for this kind of primitive version of hockey.

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:12.600
It, it only needs to have the, the triangle.

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:14.500
The rock paper scissors. Yeah, exactly.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:15.800
Like, like that works.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:19.160
So, so let's just get right into it and just talk about the face off.

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:23.380
Any, any thoughts here about like the implementation of that?

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:28.900
You know, did it feel like, did it feel like you had actually like, if I hit this button

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:31.080
at this exact time, like I'm going to steal the puck first?

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:35.060
No, to me it felt very, just mash the button as fast as you can and you probably will just

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:40.800
win. Yeah. I won 89% of my face offs, I think, just by using a skinny dude.

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:43.020
I, I don't think I won a single face off.

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:44.360
I don't know what I was doing wrong.

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:45.680
Did you pick the slow guy?

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:47.180
Uh, I'm not sure.

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:49.160
I think I just picked whoever was closest.

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:54.340
I will say it wasn't, it wasn't immediately intuitive how to, how to arrange, how to choose

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:55.820
who takes the face off.

00:10:55.980 --> 00:11:00.060
Yeah. In the manual it explains it pretty well, but, uh, but when you're just trying yourself,

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:01.380
there's like a weird, yeah.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:02.720
Yeah. Cause they cycle in and out.

00:11:02.980 --> 00:11:05.200
Swaps two players and left and right swaps the other two.

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:10.680
One and three are tied to up and down and left and right are tied to two and four.

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:13.500
And I kind of think that's fine.

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:17.500
Yeah. You know, like I wouldn't have wanted it to be like, you only cycle through the up,

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:21.360
the up button on the D pad and then you have to cycle through all four of them.

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:23.920
Yeah. I was thinking that would actually be better.

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:26.040
Just cycle, make a circle, cycle through all four of them.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:28.900
It's just like, you don't have to think about like, wait, which ones are up and down?

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:33.940
You see, I found this entire system to be completely foreign to me.

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:39.300
I didn't understand why this was a system because I don't know why you can just have your

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:44.160
positions. Like when you select your characters, like that sort of like center winger to defenseman.

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:47.060
Why wasn't it always just like that at the face off?

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:50.800
Right. Yeah. It doesn't really happen on the ice in a regular hockey game.

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:52.640
Right. Yeah. The center just takes every face.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:55.100
Send in the right wing guy. He's got this one.

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:59.620
And also it's not like you're changing who you pick based on the matchup with the computer,

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:01.580
right? You're never looking at like, oh, he sent that guy in.

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:03.020
I got to bring him. Yeah.

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:06.360
So yeah, I think that's like that.

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:10.020
Yeah. It's not the best implementation of it, but also I didn't like that.

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:13.080
You can mash the button to win the face off because I agree.

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:14.440
That's the easiest way to win the face off.

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:18.660
I remember in like for the N64 Wayne Gretzky's 3D hockey.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:22.380
If you just mash it, they won't drop the puck.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:25.860
Like they'll wait for you to settle down and then they'll drop it.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:29.140
Like you know, it's like a guy just slapping his stick against the ice.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:33.320
Yeah. You don't even see that animation in this NES game, but that's definitely asking for too

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:36.280
much. So I won't say I'm counting that against it.

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:41.560
We're not going to break down like passing and shooting and offense and defense.

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:44.240
We're not going to talk about strategies really, but I would love to talk about just

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:49.240
the core gameplay and how you guys felt about overall control of your team and shooting

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:50.140
and stuff like that.

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:56.820
So thoughts? I think it's, you kind of have to talk about that at least on a surface level.

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:01.100
If you're going to talk about how this game controls, because it's one of those games

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:09.540
sort of like soccer and also sort of like volleyball in which you, when you control your, your skater,

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:11.880
you're controlling most of the guys on the ice.

00:13:12.260 --> 00:13:14.980
Um, which is really weird to me.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:20.840
Um, because no matter what you're doing, you're controlling your goaltender while you're also

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:25.960
controlling a skater directly and most of your other teammates like indirectly.

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:29.540
Um, which can sort of cross some wires.

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:34.140
And for me, it was a very confusing way to control a game.

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:38.840
I'll say that with the controlling all the other players was a little confusing.

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:40.780
I mean, cause it's like, they're all part of one.

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:44.780
You're, you have like fine control over the one player and everyone else is part of one

00:13:44.780 --> 00:13:48.780
like blob of players that kind of moves the way that you're controlling.

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:51.460
But I liked that you were controlling the goalie at all times.

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:56.740
I, I, I would have, I wouldn't have liked to have to switch to the goalie to, to try it

00:13:56.740 --> 00:13:58.320
just before, you know, someone took a shot.

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:03.720
I had some, some issues with the goalie parts where I would try and rush and get in front

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:07.940
of a shot with a defender and then realize that that is putting my goalie completely out

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:09.580
of position and leaving the net wide open.

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:11.680
And I got scored on tons of times doing that.

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:16.020
Right. I mean, I agree that that, that that's not ideal, but I just can't think of a better solution.

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:20.140
But maybe like a, like double tap a button and it switches to the goalie automatically or

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:23.680
something like that. But to me, I feel like I, that, or at least personally, I feel like that would cause me

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:28.800
more, more problems having to, to think to switch to the goalie in time rather than just

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:32.560
to immediately be able to react by moving the goalie where I want him to go.

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:35.060
Hmm. Just because of how quickly you might have to react.

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:39.560
Gotcha. I, for me, I'm thinking of, cause you know how if you hold B then, I'm going to

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:43.040
everyone kind of rushes back to when you're on defense and you hold B, everyone rushes

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:48.540
back to your goal. I think that in that case, it makes sense to hold B and then you can take control of

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:50.800
the goalie and everyone else rushes in front of you.

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:53.820
You know? Yeah. I guess it's just an opinion.

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:55.200
Yeah. Cause yeah, I think so.

00:14:55.380 --> 00:15:01.740
A preference, I guess. I would have almost liked to have a situation where if you're at a certain point, let's say

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:07.920
you're in like the center ice, the people you're not controlling, not that they like magnetize

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:12.080
themselves to a position, but that they have a general whereabouts sense.

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:17.040
Cause it kind of seems like if you're heading in one direction, the other players on your

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:21.360
team just know to basically head in that direction with you, but don't really have any rhyme or

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:25.860
reason to like help set up a shot or like this guy's going to be kind of near the goalie or

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:28.800
something like that. If anything happens like that, it's strictly luck.

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:35.040
You know, it's not like you can go around the net, uh, from the back end, pass it to

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:37.900
a guy in front and then you take a quick slap shot, you know?

00:15:38.660 --> 00:15:44.300
Yeah. I kept having this problem where whenever the goalie, my goalie had the puck, all my guys

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:52.000
would just skate to the other side of the ring, uh, and rink and, um, and then there'd

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:54.680
just be one opponent standing right in front of the goalie.

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:58.260
And I would try and pass to someone around him and no one would be around.

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:02.520
And then it's that wires cross thing because you are still controlling a skater while you

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:05.000
have the, while you have the puck as the goalie.

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:13.840
Yeah. Um, that happened not only when I, when I would recover the, the puck, but also when I'm, you

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:19.480
know, when I'm trying to defend against this onslaught of shots and I I'm focusing on the

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:21.960
goaltender, like trying to save everything.

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:25.580
And, but I'm also trying to get like a skater in there to

00:16:25.580 --> 00:16:26.580
disrupt the play.

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:33.240
But because my, my focus is split, the, the skater I'm controlling is like just going to the

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:34.460
other end of the ice on his own.

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:40.120
Right. And you can't just, you can't just leave the goalie to his own volition because then he'll just be moving out of the way.

00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:42.300
Yeah. Yeah. It's, it's interesting.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:48.440
And how did you feel about when you are in control of a character and then you attempt to pass?

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:55.240
How did you feel about how you switch control between players there in relation to the puck being passed to you?

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:58.920
I felt like that didn't even matter because I couldn't pass correctly.

00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:00.520
Like I couldn't get accurate.

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:04.400
I wasn't born. I feel like I couldn't get accurate passes a lot of the time.

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:14.160
I think it might've been more risky, like in a, in a more traditional arcade style hockey game that you might be used to, you would have taken like bigger risks on how to pass.

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:19.320
Right. Like, oh, I see that guy all the way over on the other side, but he, but we're still on the, uh, on the offensive.

00:17:19.320 --> 00:17:25.680
So I'm going to send it over to him and the puck is just going to kind of like, you know, um, shoot over, shoot over to him.

00:17:25.820 --> 00:17:30.020
It's just going to be drawn to him where in this game you have to, you have to retrieve it.

00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:32.140
Right. Yeah. You have to kind of like go after it.

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:38.300
So I guess what I mean is, is like you kind of switch characters in the middle of a pass.

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:41.540
And so I'm wondering like, did that, did that throw you guys off?

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:49.600
Did that sometimes make it so that like you're still in control of the first character and now because you have to pick it up from the second character, you're kind of like going away from it.

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:58.540
It definitely threw me off a little bit just, just because of like prior knowledge of how other hockey, my limited prior knowledge of how other hockey games have worked in the past or in the future.

00:17:58.540 --> 00:18:10.620
Um, but, um, but I, I, I was actually going to ask if, because I wasn't, couldn't even remember if it's changes your control in the middle of the past.

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:13.000
Cause I didn't even really notice a disconnect.

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:15.600
Like I was able to quickly retrieve usually.

00:18:16.060 --> 00:18:26.720
Um, so I guess then allowing it to change control of the, of the player to the player you're passing to, or that it seems like you're passing to in the middle of the pass was really helpful.

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:28.100
Yeah. I agree.

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:36.740
Cause even though I wasn't great at passing, I think that the mechanics of controlling the next guy makes sense because after you pass with one guy, you kind of don't really care about him anymore.

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:40.040
You know, I mean, you care where he goes, you don't want to control him.

00:18:40.040 --> 00:18:42.440
So your focus would be on the guy that you're passing to.

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:52.540
And it would be a nightmare to try and have to switch players in the middle of that pass manually the way you do when you don't have the puck, because that's just, for me, it's just like, uh, just hit the, what is it?

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:55.280
The B button until you get the player you want.

00:18:55.360 --> 00:18:59.120
Like there's no really way of deciding where, which direction you're going to change to.

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:09.340
And then, you know, trying to, trying to shoot into the goal there, there's a thing there for me where like, you know, you can hold it down for like an actual slap shot.

00:19:09.460 --> 00:19:10.820
Like you see your stick go in the air.

00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:11.820
I'm cool with all that.

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:18.280
For some reason, sometimes I felt like if I just tap this thing, I wouldn't even, it wouldn't shoot the bus.

00:19:18.460 --> 00:19:19.460
That's a mechanic. I think that's a fake.

00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:20.820
Yeah. Interesting. Okay.

00:19:20.820 --> 00:19:23.360
I didn't know that until afterwards when I read the manual.

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:25.060
Don't ask me why I read the manual afterwards.

00:19:25.720 --> 00:19:29.440
But when I read the manual that you could do that, which I thought was, was interesting.

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:31.780
It, it times how quickly you press the button.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:32.920
We haven't had that yet.

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:36.180
Have we? Where you can, I guess like Mario does that.

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:42.360
Mario Jumping. A lot of games did it, but I wasn't, that's an enlightening answer from you guys that it was a fake.

00:19:42.460 --> 00:19:45.920
Cause I was always trying, cause you don't, there's no really like, it's a subtlety in the animation.

00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:48.160
So I almost felt like it was like, oh, did it not register?

00:19:48.620 --> 00:19:49.800
Because I, I don't know.

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:56.160
I feel like the fake didn't do anything to the computer opponents to be like, oh, he's, he's going to take the shot.

00:19:56.300 --> 00:20:02.660
Like let's move. It's almost like it would only be useful, I guess, if it read better and it would only work in a two player.

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:05.680
Right. One last thing on offense though.

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:17.040
If you do get it in the net, uh, the, the victory celebration is very strange because they all like go into the center ice, but they just kind of become like one blob of a thing.

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:18.500
No, they're all hugging Mike.

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:19.480
I know, but like, I don't know.

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:24.000
I would have just appreciated them like maybe skating around in a circle in the middle of the ice or something.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:25.180
Do they do that?

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:27.300
No. In real life?

00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:28.360
They don't do that.

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:30.560
You're right. They usually just pummel each other.

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:35.220
But they also don't go to the center of the ice to celebrate.

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:36.900
They just do it right then and there.

00:20:37.100 --> 00:20:37.920
The 80s were different.

00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:39.140
Yeah. They decide to meet.

00:20:39.260 --> 00:20:40.780
Like this is where we meet after we score.

00:20:40.900 --> 00:20:43.220
Yeah. That's what they're actually, that's why the face-offs there.

00:20:43.340 --> 00:20:45.400
They actually say like, when we score, we come back here.

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:46.680
Yeah. Exactly. Home base.

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:48.200
Okay. I'd buy that.

00:20:48.500 --> 00:20:59.020
Now let's talk defense because it's a little different in the sense of you can be as aggressive as you want.

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:05.700
And the only repercussion is like a fight sequence that for me was just mash buttons.

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:17.220
Yeah. That is what it is. I like that they tried to put penalties in there somehow because there's no way that you could hook somebody, stick somebody.

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:21.180
In this game, there isn't any depth of simulation for that.

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:29.880
So instead it's just like if you get into a scuffle for the puck, then immediately that means that you've done something wrong.

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:32.960
Whoever, yeah. Whoever like starts it is like, that's it.

00:21:33.140 --> 00:21:34.900
Get in the penalty box, which was cool to see.

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:39.920
I like seeing the penalty box and like actually playing four on three.

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:41.420
Yeah. No, four on five.

00:21:41.540 --> 00:21:42.680
Four on three. Yeah.

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:45.280
Okay. Cool. I liked seeing that.

00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:46.200
I thought that was a nice touch.

00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:51.160
But the fighting thing just seemed like I thought I was going to be greeted to more of like a one-on-one.

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:52.720
Not an everybody gets in.

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:54.200
It goes into punch out mode.

00:21:54.500 --> 00:21:56.140
Yeah. Right. Yeah.

00:21:56.300 --> 00:22:00.020
Or even just, you know, simple like A to punch, B to block.

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:01.620
I would have been fine with that.

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:02.620
I would have liked it.

00:22:02.660 --> 00:22:07.160
Yeah. A more direct hockey fight thing because, you know, it's not a hockey game without it.

00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:13.580
Yeah. And also, you know, and this applies to offense too, but now that I'm thinking about it, as

00:22:13.580 --> 00:22:18.840
skating physics in this game, right, the drift of like as you're moving and stuff like that,

00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:20.400
I wasn't really sold on that.

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:27.500
No. It didn't feel, it felt like they gave you maybe like an extra inch of movement after you

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:28.600
would stop your input.

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:32.420
And I just don't think like the stop on a, like I felt like it should have been a little

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:36.180
more skittish. It sort of feels like Mario was on ice more than these players.

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:39.480
Right. Yeah. Like waxy floor instead of icy floor.

00:22:40.160 --> 00:22:41.820
Yeah. Like waxy floor and socks.

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:47.460
Yeah, with socks. Exactly. And stealing the puck seemed to be like something that could happen.

00:22:47.480 --> 00:22:51.720
I never felt like the computers were like so much better because they steal the puck often.

00:22:51.860 --> 00:22:52.700
Yeah. From me at least.

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:56.360
So I kind of like was happy that if they're going to be able to steal the puck from me,

00:22:56.420 --> 00:22:58.300
at least I have a chance to get it back from them too.

00:22:58.300 --> 00:23:01.740
Yeah. Depending on what kind of player or what kind of skater you're using.

00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:03.140
Yes, that's true too.

00:23:03.620 --> 00:23:09.540
One experiment I tried later on just to see if it would work is skate around the perimeter

00:23:10.020 --> 00:23:13.920
of the ring and see if anybody would like be able to catch me.

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:16.340
The computer AI could not figure it out.

00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:19.560
They would pretty much just skate like directly behind me.

00:23:19.660 --> 00:23:22.980
And when I was going in a circle, they wouldn't try to cut me off on the back end.

00:23:23.080 --> 00:23:24.280
They would just stay behind me.

00:23:24.440 --> 00:23:25.400
It was pretty pathetic.

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:27.260
Yeah. You just got to score and then just run out the clock.

00:23:27.560 --> 00:23:29.600
Hockey players aren't really known for their intelligence.

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:31.700
Score once, run out the clock.

00:23:31.940 --> 00:23:35.800
That's the game we play. And goalie.

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:37.960
We have a goalie that you're in control of.

00:23:38.020 --> 00:23:40.420
I know you guys voice a lot of frustration over the goalie control.

00:23:40.720 --> 00:23:44.280
But did you feel like you were able to stop goals?

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:45.640
Yeah. Or was it? Yeah.

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:47.680
The guy's a brick wall if you're in front of a puck.

00:23:47.960 --> 00:23:51.320
Yeah. There's no five hole.

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:54.700
Yeah. It's a good term.

00:23:55.060 --> 00:23:58.460
Even if there's no bounce off or anything like that.

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:02.980
Oh, no. I mean, he gave up rebounds like a trampoline.

00:24:03.300 --> 00:24:08.760
Yeah. I think if the opponent fires with a slap shot, it'll bounce off you immediately.

00:24:08.920 --> 00:24:09.980
Right? No matter what.

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:11.280
That makes sense.

00:24:11.560 --> 00:24:12.600
Yeah. That's what I've been noticing.

00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:14.200
Because otherwise, I feel like I could cover it.

00:24:14.200 --> 00:24:20.100
But I feel like there were a couple of situations where there was just a guy right in front of me just firing off shots.

00:24:20.140 --> 00:24:22.300
And my goalie just wouldn't catch it.

00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:23.640
It just kept bouncing.

00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:28.940
But I feel like he was doing it so quickly and so rapidly that they couldn't have all been powerful slap shots.

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:30.520
But it might be player dependent.

00:24:30.700 --> 00:24:32.820
Maybe that was one of the husky slap shot guys.

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:33.840
Right. Husky boy.

00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:35.060
Husky boys. Yeah.

00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:38.800
Who just is better at shooting.

00:24:38.960 --> 00:24:39.860
Yeah. Like a Ty Domi.

00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:43.020
You're going to have to tell us.

00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:45.500
You're going to have to tell us all who that is.

00:24:45.680 --> 00:24:46.680
You don't know who that is?

00:24:46.780 --> 00:24:47.780
No. That's okay.

00:24:47.920 --> 00:25:07.300
All right. Great. Can we just talk about like overall presentation of this sport here in this game?

00:25:07.300 --> 00:25:12.680
Because I think, you know, baseball, when that came out, it was episode number two.

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:18.000
Right? That was very bare bones in terms of like what they were giving you.

00:25:18.220 --> 00:25:22.480
Remember, we always joked about how you can't even control the outfielders and stuff like that.

00:25:23.140 --> 00:25:27.680
Do we feel like this is doing a great job of like presenting hockey?

00:25:27.860 --> 00:25:29.760
Like the energy and rush of it all?

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:32.220
Or is it not translating well in 8-bit?

00:25:32.220 --> 00:25:41.100
Maybe it's because of baseball and my memories of that game that I was expecting this to be actually more primitive than it was.

00:25:41.280 --> 00:25:45.640
The ability to choose different players with different stats, I was not expecting in this game.

00:25:45.900 --> 00:25:49.920
But that being said, I think the rest of it is like a pretty basic example of hockey.

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:55.100
But definitely plays better than baseball, which you couldn't even field in that baseball game, right?

00:25:55.340 --> 00:25:56.780
Or was it you? No, you couldn't field it.

00:25:56.780 --> 00:26:00.920
Yeah, you couldn't control the fielders, but you did control where you threw it to.

00:26:00.980 --> 00:26:06.740
So at least in this, you can, for the most part, play the whole game and not have to just watch.

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:12.800
Yeah. I see it kind of similar to soccer with a couple, one or two things that they added.

00:26:13.180 --> 00:26:14.340
You know, that's it.

00:26:14.840 --> 00:26:16.380
Like speed and...

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:18.420
And soccer just had more flair to it.

00:26:18.660 --> 00:26:20.700
Yeah. With the halftime show and everything.

00:26:20.980 --> 00:26:22.880
Yeah. I like that we've got Zambonis in this.

00:26:23.160 --> 00:26:24.460
Yeah, they're Zambonis.

00:26:24.460 --> 00:26:27.320
I was kind of disappointed with the game.

00:26:27.660 --> 00:26:29.420
It was a little sloppy for me.

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:33.780
I guess the speed of the game is nice.

00:26:34.700 --> 00:26:37.780
That is well represented.

00:26:38.100 --> 00:26:43.580
But everything else just feels like it's nowhere near what 94 will be.

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:50.700
Right, right. And in terms of your options, too, it is nice that they give you different clock intervals and clock speed and stuff.

00:26:50.860 --> 00:26:52.680
So I appreciated all of that.

00:26:52.680 --> 00:27:04.500
I also would have liked, or rather I did like, that the different teams had preset combinations that I don't think were representative of how those teams actually play.

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:11.820
But it was just kind of cool to see, like, oh, if you select the Soviet Union, they actually have prepared big guys, you know, like big thugs.

00:27:11.820 --> 00:27:16.020
And then, like, if you pick, like, Canada, it's a bunch of speedy guys.

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:18.240
And if you pick Poland, I forget.

00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:21.120
It's, like, more a mishmash of everybody.

00:27:21.420 --> 00:27:25.220
So I like that those things were done for this game.

00:27:25.220 --> 00:27:36.680
But I almost wish that there was, you know, like a little more flair to the whole game in general and not just, like, well, we've constructed as much of the ice hockey portion as we can.

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:38.920
So let's not worry so much about, like.

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:42.620
So you want, like, different arenas for each team?

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:44.420
Or what are you talking about?

00:27:44.420 --> 00:27:46.640
Yeah, no, I think that's, yeah, I think that's kind of what I'm getting at.

00:27:46.700 --> 00:27:58.220
Or maybe even just, like, more time spent on, like, the presentation of the teams, you know, like USA and actually seeing, like, a red, white, and blue jersey on them.

00:27:58.380 --> 00:28:03.920
Or, like, you know, a crowd that actually, like, has some things going on in it, you know?

00:28:04.340 --> 00:28:05.160
They turn their heads.

00:28:05.200 --> 00:28:07.500
I was just going to say, they look at each other once in a while.

00:28:07.680 --> 00:28:12.200
But we've kind of, like, seen elements of these things in all of Nintendo's sports games.

00:28:12.200 --> 00:28:14.960
So I feel like it's just a matter of time until they all become one.

00:28:15.060 --> 00:28:19.340
But I'm not so sold that it's going to happen in a Nintendo-developed sports game.

00:28:19.580 --> 00:28:21.720
Yeah. So, like, somebody is going to crack this code.

00:28:22.740 --> 00:28:30.740
It's just not going to be Nintendo because it seems like every single time we get, like, one push forward but some forgotten lessons along the way, too.

00:28:30.740 --> 00:28:41.420
We get more a representation of the rules of a sport, you know, sometimes better than others, and not so much, like, the spirit of the sport.

00:28:41.620 --> 00:28:43.280
Yes. Which I think this sort of misses.

00:28:43.560 --> 00:28:45.080
Fair. Which is, I think, what I was trying to say.

00:28:45.120 --> 00:28:47.160
Yeah. I appreciate you throwing that out there.

00:28:47.540 --> 00:28:48.580
Set up better than me. For sure.

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:51.220
Did you guys know that there are codes for this game?

00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:52.440
No. Cheat codes that you can put in.

00:28:52.440 --> 00:28:53.320
Did not know that. Okay.

00:28:53.860 --> 00:28:56.920
The first one is a no goalies code.

00:28:58.260 --> 00:29:04.560
On the title screen, press and hold the A button and the B button on both the first and second control pad.

00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:11.180
Then, press the start button on the first control pad, and when the game starts, neither team will have goaltenders.

00:29:11.360 --> 00:29:13.560
You'll just be playing four on four.

00:29:13.820 --> 00:29:17.260
Did every NES come with two control pads, or did you have to buy the second one?

00:29:17.260 --> 00:29:18.420
For the most part, they actually did. Okay.

00:29:18.420 --> 00:29:20.460
It was like a selling point of the system.

00:29:20.700 --> 00:29:23.460
It would be funny if they made you buy the second controller.

00:29:23.660 --> 00:29:29.600
It's like, press the on button on your Rob on the title screen.

00:29:30.400 --> 00:29:36.720
I actually know this because the Famicom version had the controllers built in to the system.

00:29:37.140 --> 00:29:40.260
They actually played on the side, kind of like how it switches.

00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:42.540
You put them on the side of the console.

00:29:42.780 --> 00:29:49.400
The Famicom has controller pad rests on each side, and a wire that doesn't really extend too far.

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:51.280
But it also can unplug it, though, right?

00:29:51.700 --> 00:29:53.380
That I'm not 100% sure on.

00:29:53.420 --> 00:29:55.680
I think that's a Famicom disk system thing.

00:29:55.680 --> 00:29:59.760
Is that the disk system added the ability to plug in controller ports.

00:29:59.940 --> 00:30:03.680
Pretty sure. But for the Famicom one, it was wired and connected the whole time.

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:07.220
That's like one bad controller can ruin the entire system?

00:30:07.380 --> 00:30:08.620
Yeah. Wow. That's kind of crazy.

00:30:09.400 --> 00:30:12.060
And then there's also the frictionless puck.

00:30:12.120 --> 00:30:14.320
Cheat. It's the only other cheat.

00:30:14.600 --> 00:30:15.940
There it is. Lunar hockey.

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:17.080
Yeah, you did some lunar hockey.

00:30:17.460 --> 00:30:20.580
On the team select screen, highlight the team you wish to control.

00:30:20.820 --> 00:30:23.940
Then press and hold the A button and B button on both control pads.

00:30:23.940 --> 00:30:32.580
Then press start on the first control pad to cause the puck to continue moving until it is brought under control by a player or a goal is scored.

00:30:32.860 --> 00:30:42.920
If at any time the puck becomes airborne, it will continue to fly around the rim until it touches someone to bring it to the ice level or enter the net of a goal.

00:30:43.040 --> 00:30:45.800
So it's also a gravity-less puck.

00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:51.880
Yeah, so it essentially will just stay in the air, which is something that happened for me more on passes than anything.

00:30:52.060 --> 00:30:55.860
Like sometimes my passes would like just kind of fly up into the air.

00:30:55.980 --> 00:30:59.720
I was like, is that because I'm like holding it down too long or something?

00:30:59.840 --> 00:31:00.660
I don't know. I wasn't sure.

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:05.120
Sure. So there isn't really a sequels and spinoffs section here today.

00:31:05.900 --> 00:31:10.160
There are other hockey games that we'll be playing even pretty soon, honestly.

00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:16.980
Okay. But I didn't want to talk about those today or even name drop them because that's a thing for like future episodes.

00:31:17.260 --> 00:31:24.580
Instead, I want to talk about the only two Japanese-only hockey games that will come to the NES.

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:26.920
And I actually got a chance to play them.

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:29.140
So I'll give you like just a little, you know, a couple thoughts.

00:31:29.480 --> 00:31:33.540
But the first one was Stick Hunter Exciting Ice Hockey.

00:31:33.760 --> 00:31:34.600
Stick Hunter.

00:31:34.700 --> 00:31:38.520
What? Stick Hunter Exciting Ice Hockey.

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:40.320
His name, Stick Hunter.

00:31:40.320 --> 00:31:45.140
Yeah. And for me here, this one is very similar to Ice Hockey.

00:31:45.180 --> 00:31:51.840
And I actually think might have come first with the only exception being that the graphics are a little more detailed.

00:31:52.220 --> 00:31:54.240
Okay. It actually has a bigger rink.

00:31:54.340 --> 00:31:57.900
It has the, you know, like all the zones and stuff like that.

00:31:57.900 --> 00:32:00.320
You can take a face off in each, you know.

00:32:00.320 --> 00:32:01.500
Yeah, you can do that in this one too.

00:32:01.620 --> 00:32:03.660
Okay. Sorry. I forgot that detail then.

00:32:03.780 --> 00:32:10.120
Yeah. But overall, it feels like a clone of this game in many regards.

00:32:11.100 --> 00:32:17.000
And the goalie can take like, can take the hit of a puck.

00:32:17.320 --> 00:32:19.840
And then like it can still like bounce behind him.

00:32:20.040 --> 00:32:20.920
Okay. And go in the net.

00:32:21.040 --> 00:32:25.100
I like that. So, I guess that's that part that makes it exciting.

00:32:25.320 --> 00:32:28.760
It's very exciting. It's very Stick Hunter. That's why I guess the name Exciting Ice Hockey instead.

00:32:29.660 --> 00:32:41.920
Now, the second game, I'm kind of sad that we don't get, and that I could only play in Japanese because I'm sure a translation exists somewhere, but I could only play it in the Japanese text version.

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:46.320
It's Crash and the Boys Ice Challenge.

00:32:46.760 --> 00:32:55.500
Who's Crash? Now, I'm glad that you asked who Crash is because Crash takes place in the Renegade universe.

00:32:55.960 --> 00:32:58.980
Remember we just played Renegade, the beat-em-up game?

00:32:59.160 --> 00:33:00.740
Okay. They play hockey now.

00:33:00.740 --> 00:33:02.260
Yeah, they play hockey now.

00:33:02.460 --> 00:33:04.960
And it's in that whole like world and aesthetic.

00:33:04.960 --> 00:33:05.800
Is that like street hockey?

00:33:05.980 --> 00:33:07.180
No, it's ice hockey.

00:33:07.180 --> 00:33:09.420
And you pick your team and everything like that.

00:33:09.520 --> 00:33:11.520
Like you choose your players and everything.

00:33:11.640 --> 00:33:14.180
And they have like a little more detailed stats to be honest.

00:33:14.700 --> 00:33:17.020
But there's also like a story mode behind it.

00:33:17.100 --> 00:33:22.060
Like today you're playing this team and there's a whole like, you know, exchange and cut scene and stuff.

00:33:22.240 --> 00:33:25.200
And then like I didn't understand what they were talking about, but then you play that team.

00:33:26.180 --> 00:33:30.860
Because it was Japanese. And the final score for me in the very first game, I won 16-2.

00:33:31.200 --> 00:33:38.480
Oh my God. It's so easy to score goals that I actually stopped scoring goals at a point and just tried to like try everything else in the game.

00:33:38.560 --> 00:33:39.780
That's how they scored the other two goals.

00:33:39.780 --> 00:33:44.540
It was kind of pathetic that you can just win the puck on faceoff, which you can't win by mashing.

00:33:44.920 --> 00:33:48.820
Instead, the referee just drops the puck.

00:33:49.100 --> 00:33:53.640
And then as soon as the puck is dropped, both players just can charge towards the puck.

00:33:53.640 --> 00:33:55.660
Okay, so this is like XFL hockey.

00:33:55.860 --> 00:34:01.760
Right, right. And then assuming you get control, you can immediately just like make a straight line for the goalie.

00:34:02.600 --> 00:34:04.700
Just pivot either left or right a little bit.

00:34:04.820 --> 00:34:06.020
And I guarantee you're getting a goal.

00:34:06.340 --> 00:34:09.780
Wow. I'm sure it gets harder later on in the story.

00:34:09.900 --> 00:34:12.420
Yeah. But I do kind of want to see where the story goes, right?

00:34:12.500 --> 00:34:15.880
Is it just like a, you know, high school rivalry story?

00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:19.260
Or is it like eventually you'll fight like the demon hockey team?

00:34:19.260 --> 00:34:21.060
And what connects it to Renegade?

00:34:21.180 --> 00:34:22.200
Are you that character?

00:34:22.420 --> 00:34:28.720
Well, yeah. I remember we talked about this in Renegade that there was a whole thing where like in Japan, it's different enemies.

00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:30.060
Yeah, it's not warriors.

00:34:30.360 --> 00:34:31.360
Right. It's not.

00:34:31.520 --> 00:34:33.840
Yeah, it's not. We Americanized it for Renegade.

00:34:33.840 --> 00:34:38.520
Right, that's right. It's a whole series of, what is that, River City Ransom, right?

00:34:38.720 --> 00:34:39.720
Oh, yeah. Yeah.

00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:41.000
Oh, that connects to River City.

00:34:41.020 --> 00:34:42.100
Did we talk about that right again?

00:34:42.100 --> 00:34:43.220
Yeah, we did. Oh, man. I wasn't paying attention.

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:55.320
Yeah. I do have one straight thought, and it is in regards to the pause sound of this game.

00:34:55.640 --> 00:34:57.200
Okay. And it's been a little while since I've done a pause sound.

00:34:57.320 --> 00:35:05.120
It's not anything too extensive, but the pause sound, correct me if I'm wrong, I think is exactly the same as the Super Mario Bros.

00:35:05.200 --> 00:35:07.660
pause sound. I never paused.

00:35:08.360 --> 00:35:12.960
I paused, and I remember it being familiar, but I didn't place it at the time.

00:35:13.020 --> 00:35:14.060
Is it just da-da-da-da? Yeah.

00:35:14.460 --> 00:35:15.480
Okay. Huh. Yeah.

00:35:16.040 --> 00:35:17.060
That's really it. Okay.

00:35:17.060 --> 00:35:19.820
I'll confirm on the Best of 88 episode if it is.

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:22.220
You should get that notarized, too.

00:35:22.600 --> 00:35:23.420
Yes, of course.

00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:24.500
It's official. Are you kidding?

00:35:24.800 --> 00:35:26.240
Yeah. That's all I've got.

00:35:26.500 --> 00:35:28.960
You would think I have more, but I don't.

00:35:29.800 --> 00:35:32.280
Go Rangers. No, it's go Sabres.

00:35:32.520 --> 00:35:34.680
Interesting. Sam, do you want to root for a hockey team?

00:35:34.960 --> 00:35:36.920
I go Devils because I'm from New Jersey.

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:38.940
Cool. Yeah. I got to go Sabres.

00:35:39.140 --> 00:35:41.160
Okay. Yeah. It's the only team we have.

00:35:41.320 --> 00:35:47.020
Come on. Anyway, we have to give the fans what they want, and that is the Essential Games List.

00:35:47.220 --> 00:35:55.020
Oh. There's no real way to get into this better than to just ask straight up for your guys' opinions.

00:35:55.800 --> 00:36:00.000
Everybody knows what the Essential Games List is by now, and no one's joining us for the

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:01.540
first time on an ice hockey episode.

00:36:02.060 --> 00:36:03.360
So go ahead, Joe.

00:36:03.460 --> 00:36:09.200
What are your thoughts? It's already hard for a sports game to get into the Essential Games List, in my opinion,

00:36:09.460 --> 00:36:10.880
because they're all very similar.

00:36:11.060 --> 00:36:12.100
They're all doing the same thing.

00:36:12.420 --> 00:36:15.980
And this one, while I did think it was better than I thought it was going to be, it was okay.

00:36:16.240 --> 00:36:19.480
It didn't do anything that special where I'm going to vote it in.

00:36:19.840 --> 00:36:27.900
Okay. This was a disappointing game to me because I do love me some hockey, and I just thought

00:36:27.900 --> 00:36:34.440
it was too sloppy of a representation of the sport to say anything aside from it is inessential.

00:36:34.720 --> 00:36:36.720
I don't really have much else to say.

00:36:37.020 --> 00:36:43.420
This game just kind of frustrated the hell out of me, and I blame myself a little bit

00:36:43.420 --> 00:36:47.400
for that, for not being good at it, but also I blame the game for that, for not being that

00:36:47.400 --> 00:36:48.560
good of a game.

00:36:48.680 --> 00:36:50.500
So it's not essential. All right.

00:36:50.780 --> 00:36:53.040
And just my final thoughts on this.

00:36:53.180 --> 00:36:57.700
I think everyone has said mostly what everyone probably feels about this game.

00:36:57.820 --> 00:37:03.300
I'm a little surprised to find out that IGN had this at number 100 on their top 100 NES

00:37:03.300 --> 00:37:05.140
games. Yeah, I read that.

00:37:05.240 --> 00:37:06.140
And I was like, what?

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:08.080
Yeah. All right. Cool, I guess.

00:37:08.080 --> 00:37:12.560
To me, there's something about this game that we never talked about in the episode, but

00:37:12.560 --> 00:37:14.760
that I felt throughout every time I-

00:37:14.760 --> 00:37:16.340
We're still in the episode. No, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:37:16.380 --> 00:37:20.240
But I meant like I never really conveyed this feeling, so I don't want to surprise listeners

00:37:20.240 --> 00:37:24.500
with it, but basically every time I picked up this game, I almost like had the urge to

00:37:24.500 --> 00:37:25.480
stop immediately.

00:37:25.800 --> 00:37:29.660
And I didn't really have that with most NES games.

00:37:29.900 --> 00:37:30.780
It's just frustrating to play. Right.

00:37:30.860 --> 00:37:31.680
Even the bad ones.

00:37:31.800 --> 00:37:36.040
There was just something about like I never really wanted to play through like a full-

00:37:36.040 --> 00:37:38.640
And you're not even like 15 minutes each period.

00:37:38.840 --> 00:37:41.820
Like it's the abbreviated like super speed, seven minutes, whatever.

00:37:42.240 --> 00:37:43.180
It's 20. It's supposed to be.

00:37:43.280 --> 00:37:44.380
Yeah, yeah, yeah. But you know what I'm saying.

00:37:44.520 --> 00:37:45.880
Like across each period.

00:37:46.040 --> 00:37:49.400
And it's like I never even really wanted to play like a full three-period game.

00:37:50.120 --> 00:37:52.420
And so I just kind of played this game in bursts.

00:37:53.120 --> 00:37:55.700
And it never really connected for me.

00:37:55.780 --> 00:37:57.140
So I'm not sure if I gave it.

00:37:57.300 --> 00:37:59.780
You missed out on how much better it gets in the third period.

00:38:00.120 --> 00:38:02.000
Yeah, you never saw the Zambonis.

00:38:02.060 --> 00:38:04.120
Also, we didn't mention Shootout.

00:38:04.120 --> 00:38:05.780
You might not have gotten to the Shootout.

00:38:06.140 --> 00:38:07.520
I never got to the Shootout.

00:38:07.600 --> 00:38:08.520
Or there's also Overtime.

00:38:08.620 --> 00:38:11.160
There's Overtime after the Shootout, which is not accurate.

00:38:11.300 --> 00:38:12.700
A goalie-less Overtime.

00:38:12.840 --> 00:38:15.580
Yeah. There's Overtime after the Shootout?

00:38:15.580 --> 00:38:19.180
Yeah, a Shootout in the first tiebreaker and then Overtime.

00:38:19.320 --> 00:38:20.440
Did this happen in the 80s?

00:38:21.020 --> 00:38:23.840
I don't. Maybe this is some international thing.

00:38:24.220 --> 00:38:28.400
I don't think that goalie-less hockey is ever an official hockey.

00:38:28.460 --> 00:38:32.080
And hockey's only just getting good for what it's worth in this time.

00:38:32.080 --> 00:38:40.080
1987, so it's just getting good because Wayne Gretzky at this point is reinventing hockey single-handedly

00:38:40.080 --> 00:38:42.720
and changing what it means for the game to do anything.

00:38:42.860 --> 00:38:48.920
Because before that, if you actually look at 1950s footage of hockey, it's okay.

00:38:49.220 --> 00:38:51.040
The goalies don't know how to skate.

00:38:51.680 --> 00:38:56.680
They essentially are just those brick walls you're talking about where they just stand there

00:38:56.680 --> 00:39:00.320
and then you shoot and then they just pretend like there's anything they can do about it.

00:39:00.800 --> 00:39:05.500
I don't know. I think that there is something endearing about old hockey footage.

00:39:05.720 --> 00:39:14.640
But the thing about this game that I truly disliked is it did not convey entirely how much the Leafs suck.

